{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1791297347355,"sessionID":"ses_eee5d3465ffeQIkXyaRSImUGGH","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"write","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-90db4608db18205d","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-kalepail-Desktop-kalepail-skills/fd5aae97-76bc-4bb3-8597-af0fa91bb3f0/scratchpad/probe-runs/glmflash-max__prose__r1__1791297243/work/rewritten.txt","content":"The team upgraded the fleet daemon to v4.12.0 last quarter. Before that, the routing table lived in several scattered files. Staff merged those files into one file at /etc/fleetd/routes.toml. The process reads that file once at startup. It then caches the routing table in memory. The cache stays valid until the process restarts or receives a reload signal from the supervisor. Operators used to edit the old files directly. The team advised these operators to validate each change first. They should run the reload command with the --dry-run flag. A malformed entry can slip past an unchecked reload. Then every worker in the pool falls back to the default route. The incident review measured that fallback. It cost roughly $0.35 per thousand requests in extra upstream charges. The reload command prints a diff of the routes that would change. It exits with a nonzero status when it cannot parse an entry. You can wire it into a pre-commit hook without much effort. The system automatically migrated older entries written for the previous schema. The system dropped entries that referenced retired regions. It logged each drop as a warning.\n"},"output":"Wrote file successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"filepath":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-kalepail-Desktop-kalepail-skills/fd5aae97-76bc-4bb3-8597-af0fa91bb3f0/scratchpad/probe-runs/glmflash-max__prose__r1__1791297243/work/rewritten.txt","exists":false,"truncated":false},"title":"private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-kalepail-Desktop-kalepail-skills/fd5aae97-76bc-4bb3-8597-af0fa91bb3f0/scratchpad/probe-runs/glmflash-max__prose__r1__1791297243/work/rewritten.txt","time":{"start":1791297347338,"end":1791297347344}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Let me analyze the source paragraph carefully to keep every fact.\n\nOriginal text:\n\n\"When the fleet daemon was upgraded to v4.12.0 last quarter, the routing table that had previously been stored in several scattered files was consolidated into a single file at /etc/fleetd/routes.toml, which is read once at startup and then cached in memory until the process is restarted or a reload signal is received from the supervisor. Operators who were accustomed to editing the old files directly were advised that any change should first be validated by running the reload command with the --dry-run flag, because a malformed entry that was not caught before the reload would cause every worker in the pool to fall back to the default route, and that fallback was found during the incident review to cost roughly $0.35 per thousand requests in extra upstream charges. The reload command prints a diff of the routes that would change, and it exits with a nonzero status if any entry cannot be parsed, so it can be wired into a pre-commit hook without much effort. Older entries that were written for the previous schema were automatically migrated, but entries that referenced retired regions were dropped and logged as warnings.\"\n\nFacts to preserve:\n1. Fleet daemon upgraded to v4.12.0 last quarter.\n2. Routing table previously stored in several scattered files.\n3. Consolidated into a single file at /etc/fleetd/routes.toml.\n4. File read once at startup, then cached in memory until process restart or a reload signal from the supervisor.\n5. Operators accustomed to editing old files directly were advised: any change should first be validated by running the reload command with --dry-run flag.\n6. Reason: a malformed entry not caught before reload would cause every worker in the pool to fall back to the default route.\n7. Incident review found that fallback cost roughly $0.35 per thousand requests in extra upstream charges.\n8. Reload command prints a diff of routes that would change.\n9. It exits with nonzero status if any entry cannot be parsed.\n10. So it can be wired into a pre-commit hook without much effort.\n11. Older entries written for previous schema automatically migrated.\n12. Entries referencing retired regions were dropped and logged as warnings.\n\nProtected tokens: /etc/fleetd/routes.toml, --dry-run, v4.12.0, $0.35. Keep exactly as written.\n\nRules:\n1. Every sentence ≤ 20 words (whitespace-separated tokens; sentence ends with . ! or ?).\n2. No passive: no \"was\"/\"were\"/\"been\" followed (optionally after one adverb ending in -ly) by a word ending in \"ed\".\n\nThe regex presumably is something like \\b(was|were|been)\\b(\\s+\\w+ly)?\\s+\\w+ed\\b. I must avoid this entirely. So I should avoid passive voice: rewrite in active voice. Note: any \"was/were/been + adverb-ly + -ed word\" also banned. So I should just avoid using \"was\", \"were\", \"been\" before any -ed word. Safer: avoid \"was\", \"were\", \"been\" entirely, or at least never place them before -ed words.\n\nAlso careful: \"ed\" ending — words like \"passed\", \"processed\", \"used\", \"based\", \"needed\", \"received\". So if I write \"was not caught\" — caught doesn't end in \"ed\"... but rule says word ending in \"ed\". \"caught\" doesn't end with ed. But \"was read\" — \"read\" doesn't end in ed. Hmm, but let's be safe and avoid passive-ish constructs with was/were/been entirely.\n\nAlso note \"ed\" ending includes things like \"needed\", \"wanted\". Let me just write entirely active-voice prose and avoid was/were/been entirely.\n\nAlso \"had been\" — banned combo if followed by -ed word. Avoid.\n\nSentences ≤ 20 words each. Count whitespace-separated tokens. Punctuation: sentence ends with . ! or ?. Careful: \"v4.12.0\" contains periods — a naive checker might split at those periods! The rule says \"a sentence ends with . ! or ?\" — the checker likely uses regex to split on [\\.!?]. If it splits on every period, \"v4.12.0\" would break sentences weirdly. Hmm. But the protected tokens must be kept exactly. The checker presumably accounts for token counting as whitespace-separated tokens and sentences end with . ! or ?. If the checker splits on every \".\", then \"v4.12.0\" would create tiny \"sentences\" like \"When the fleet daemon upgraded to v4\" (7 words), \"12\" (1 word), \"0 last quarter...\" — each of those fragments would need ≤20 words. As long as each fragment is short, that's fine. The fragments created by periods inside v4.12.0 will be short, so even a naive split passes. Let me be careful: any sentence containing \"v4.12.0\" — the split pieces must each be ≤20 words. If I write \"The team upgraded the fleet daemon to v4.12.0 last quarter.\" — naive split: \"The team upgraded the fleet daemon to v4\" (8 words), \"12\" (1), \"0 last quarter.\" (3). Fine.\n\nSimilarly \"/etc/fleetd/routes.toml\" has dots. Naive split on \".\" would fragment. Keep sentences containing it short. E.g., \"The routing table now lives in one file: /etc/fleetd/routes.toml.\" Naive split: \"The routing table now lives in one file: /etc/fleetd/routes\" (9 words), \"toml.\" (1). Fine. But careful: \"routes\" — wait, does the no-passive rule apply across fragments? The regex for passive: \"was/were/been followed (optionally after one -ly adverb) by a word ending in ed\". That's likely a regex like (was|were|been)\\s+(\\w+ly\\s+)?\\w+ed\\b. It searches text, doesn't care about sentence boundaries. So I just need to avoid those patterns anywhere.\n\nAlso potential issue: words ending in \"ed\" followed... no wait, rule 2 is only about was/were/been. Fine.\n\nWhat about \"ed\" in \"v4.12.0\"? No. \"$0.35\" has a period too! Naive split on \".\" would break \"0\" and \"35\". Keep those sentences short: e.g., \"...costs roughly $0.35 per thousand requests.\" Naive split: fragment ending at \"costs roughly $0\" — then \"35 per thousand requests.\" — both short. Fine.\n\nAlso the word \"ed\" ending check: regex \\w+ed\\b probably matches \"ed\" at word end. Words like \"red\", \"bed\", \"speed\" don't end in \"ed\"? \"speed\" ends in \"ed\"! \"was\" followed by \"speed\" → banned. But I'm avoiding was/were/been entirely, so no issue.\n\nNow, also consider: does the checker treat \".\" inside \"routes.toml\" as a sentence end? If so, fragments like \"toml.\" count as a sentence with 1 word — fine. But also a concern: if a fragment like \"The file sits at /etc/fleetd/routes\" — that's fine too.\n\nAnother subtlety: rule 1 says words are whitespace-separated tokens. So \"$0.35\" is one token. Sentence counting: the checker probably splits text into sentences by [.!?] and counts whitespace tokens per sentence. To be safe, keep each sentence well under 20 words, ideally ≤15, so even naive splits pass.\n\nLet me draft the rewrite:\n\nOriginal facts again:\n\nDraft:\n\n\"The team upgraded the fleet daemon to v4.12.0 last quarter.\" (10 words) ✓ active, no was/were/been.\n\n\"Before that, the routing table lived in several scattered files.\" (10 words) ✓\n\n\"Staff merged those files into one file at /etc/fleetd/routes.toml.\" Naive split: \"Staff merged those files into one file at /etc/fleetd/routes\" (10 words) + \"toml.\" (1 word). ✓ Both ≤20.\n\n\"The process reads that file once at startup.\" (8 words) ✓\n\n\"It then caches the table in memory.\" (6 words) ✓\n\n\"The cache stays valid until the process restarts or receives a reload signal from the supervisor.\" (15 words) ✓\n\n\"Operators used to edit the old files directly.\" (8 words) ✓\n\n\"The team advised these operators to validate each change first.\" (10 words) ✓ — \"advised\" — no passive issue since no was/were/been before it.\n\n\"They should run the reload command with the --dry-run flag.\" (10 words) ✓\n\n\"A malformed entry can slip past a direct reload.\" (9 words) ✓\n\n\"Then every worker in the pool falls back to the default route.\" (11 words) ✓\n\n\"The incident review measured that fallback.\" (6 words) ✓\n\n\"It cost roughly $0.35 per thousand requests in extra upstream charges.\" Naive split: \"It cost roughly $0\" (4), \"35 per thousand requests in extra upstream charges.\" (7). ✓\n\nWait — need to double check rule 2 more carefully. Regex: (was|were|been)\\s+(?:\\w+ly\\s+)?\\w+ed\\b — I'm not using was/were/been anywhere, so fine. But let me double-check every word I use: \"advised\" is fine (not after was/were/been). \"started\" etc. fine.\n\nHmm, one more consideration: does the checker maybe check for \"is/are + ed\"? Rule says only \"was\", \"were\", \"been\". But \"is received\" — \"received\" ends in \"ed\" but preceded by \"is\", which is allowed. Still, to be safe with \"plain prose\", I'll keep everything active anyway.\n\nContinue:\n\n\"The reload command prints a diff of the routes that would change.\" (12 words) ✓ — \"would change\" fine.\n\n\"It exits with a nonzero status if any entry cannot be parsed.\" Hmm \"be parsed\" — \"be\" is not banned. But \"cannot be parsed\" is passive-ish; allowed by the letter of the rule (be is not was/were/been). But maybe the checker also looks for \"be + ed\"? Rule says only was/were/been. To be extra safe, rephrase: \"It exits with a nonzero status when it cannot parse an entry.\" (12 words) ✓ Active.\n\nHmm wait, \"cannot parse\" — active. Good.\n\n\"So you can wire it into a pre-commit hook without much effort.\" Naive split on \".\"? No periods inside except final. (12 words) ✓. Hmm \"So\" starting a sentence is fine. Or \"You can wire it into a pre-commit hook without much effort.\" (11 words) ✓\n\n\"Older entries written for the previous schema migrated automatically.\" Hmm, \"migrated\" — intransitive active use: \"Older entries ... migrated automatically\" — that's active (migrate can be intransitive). 9 words. ✓ Or \"The system migrated older entries for the previous schema automatically.\" (10 words) ✓ Let me keep the fact: entries written for previous schema were automatically migrated. Active: \"The system migrated older entries that matched the previous schema.\" Wait the fact is \"older entries that were written for the previous schema\". So: \"The system migrated older entries that match the previous schema.\" Hmm, \"written for\" — I can say \"The system migrated older entries from the previous schema.\" (9 words) — keeps meaning. Or \"The system automatically migrated older entries written for the previous schema.\" (10 words) — \"written\" is fine (not after was/were/been). ✓\n\n\"But the system dropped entries that referenced retired regions and logged warnings.\" Hmm — fact: \"entries that referenced retired regions were dropped and logged as warnings.\" Active: \"The system dropped entries that referenced retired regions and logged warnings.\" (11 words) ✓ Or \"The system dropped and logged warnings for entries that referenced retired regions.\" Slightly changes meaning — logged as warnings refers to the entries being dropped (log messages). \"The system dropped entries that referenced retired regions. It logged each drop as a warning.\" Two sentences. Fine.\n\nLet me now also double check no sentence exceeds 20 words and no passive regex matches. Also ensure protected tokens exact: \"/etc/fleetd/routes.toml\" — I wrote \"/etc/fleetd/routes.toml\" — wait, in my draft I wrote \"at /etc/fleetd/routes.toml\" — hmm, original says \"at /etc/fleetd/routes.toml\". Hmm, actually original: \"consolidated into a single file at /etc/fleetd/routes.toml\". Hmm wait, actually it says \"/etc/fleetd/routes.toml\". Let me re-read: \"a single file at /etc/fleetd/routes.toml\" — yes. The protected token list says: /etc/fleetd/routes.toml  --dry-run  v4.12.0  $0.35. So I must include \"/etc/fleetd/routes.toml\" exactly.\n\nHmm, careful: I typed \"routes.toml\" — the token is \"routes.toml\". Good.\n\n\"--dry-run\" exact. \"v4.12.0\" exact. \"$0.35\" exact.\n\nNow assemble the full paragraph and count each sentence (using both real sentence ends and naive period splits):\n\nDraft full text:\n\n\"The team upgraded the fleet daemon to v4.12.0 last quarter. Before that, the routing table lived in several scattered files. Staff merged those files into one file at /etc/fleetd/routes.toml. The process reads that file once at startup. It then caches the routing table in memory. The cache stays valid until the process restarts or receives a reload signal from the supervisor. Operators used to edit the old files directly. The team advised these operators to validate each change first. They should run the reload command with the --dry-run flag. A malformed entry can slip past a direct reload. Then every worker in the pool falls back to the default route. The incident review measured that fallback. It cost roughly $0.35 per thousand requests in extra upstream charges. The reload command prints a diff of the routes that would change. It exits with a nonzero status when it cannot parse an entry. You can wire it into a pre-commit hook without much effort. The system automatically migrated older entries written for the previous schema. The system dropped entries that referenced retired regions. It logged each drop as a warning.\"\n\nCheck sentence lengths (real sentences):\n\n1. \"The team upgraded the fleet daemon to v4.12.0 last quarter.\" → tokens: The(1) team(2) upgraded(3) the(4) fleet(5) daemon(6) to(7) v4.12.0(8) last(9) quarter.(10) = 10 ✓\n2. \"Before that, the routing table lived in several scattered files.\" = Before(1) that,(2) the(3) routing(4) table(5) lived(6) in(7) several(8) scattered(9) files.(10) = 10 ✓\n3. \"Staff merged those files into one file at /etc/fleetd/routes.toml.\" = Staff(1) merged(2) those(3) files(4) into(5) one(6) file(7) at(8) /etc/fleetd/routes.toml.(9) = 9 ✓\n\nHmm wait — should I end the sentence with \"/etc/fleetd/routes.toml.\" — the period after .toml is a sentence end. The token itself is \"/etc/fleetd/routes.toml\" and the trailing \".\" is sentence punctuation. The protected token remains exact within text. Fine.\n\nBut naive split: \"Staff merged those files into one file at /etc/fleetd/routes\" (9 tokens incl \"routes\") then \"toml.\" (1). ✓\n\n4. \"The process reads that file once at startup.\" = 8 ✓\n5. \"It then caches the routing table in memory.\" = 8 ✓\n6. \"The cache stays valid until the process restarts or receives a reload signal from the supervisor.\" = The(1) cache(2) stays(3) valid(4) until(5) the(6) process(7) restarts(8) or(9) receives(10) a(11) reload(12) signal(13) from(14) the(15) supervisor.(16) = 16 ✓\n7. \"Operators used to edit the old files directly.\" = 8 ✓\n8. \"The team advised these operators to validate each change first.\" = 10 ✓\n9. \"They should run the reload command with the --dry-run flag.\" = They(1) should(2) run(3) the(4) reload(5) command(6) with(7) the(8) --dry-run(9) flag.(10) = 10 ✓\n10. \"A malformed entry can slip past a direct reload.\" = 9 ✓\n11. \"Then every worker in the pool falls back to the default route.\" = 12 ✓\n12. \"The incident review measured that fallback.\" = 6 ✓\n13. \"It cost roughly $0.35 per thousand requests in extra upstream charges.\" = It(1) cost(2) roughly(3) $0.35(4) per(5) thousand(6) requests(7) in(8) extra(9) upstream(10) charges.(11) = 11 ✓\n\nNaive split: \"It cost roughly $0\" (4) / \"35 per thousand requests in extra upstream charges.\" (7) ✓\n\n14. \"The reload command prints a diff of the routes that would change.\" = 12 ✓\n15. \"It exits with a nonzero status when it cannot parse an entry.\" = 12 ✓\n16. \"You can wire it into a pre-commit hook without much effort.\" = 11 ✓\n17. \"The system automatically migrated older entries written for the previous schema.\" = 10 ✓\n18. \"The system dropped entries that referenced retired regions.\" = 8 ✓\n19. \"It logged each drop as a warning.\" = 7 ✓\n\nFact check against original:\n1. upgraded to v4.12.0 last quarter ✓ (S1)\n2. routing table previously stored in several scattered files ✓ (S2: \"Before that\" = previously; \"lived in several scattered files\") ✓\n3. consolidated into a single file at /etc/fleetd/routes.toml ✓ (S3)\n4. read once at startup, cached in memory until process restart or reload signal from supervisor ✓ (S4–S6)\n5. operators accustomed to editing old files directly advised to validate first via reload --dry-run ✓ (S7–S9). Original: \"were advised that any change should first be validated by running the reload command with the --dry-run flag\". Mine: operators used to edit old files; team advised them to validate each change first; run reload command with --dry-run. ✓\n6. malformed entry not caught before reload → every worker falls back to default route ✓ (S10–S11). Original nuance: \"a malformed entry that was not caught before the reload would cause every worker in the pool to fall back to the default route\". Mine: \"A malformed entry can slip past a direct reload. Then every worker in the pool falls back to the default route.\" ✓ keeps the causal link.\n7. incident review found fallback cost roughly $0.35 per thousand requests in extra upstream charges ✓ (S12–S13)\n8. reload command prints diff of routes that would change ✓ (S14)\n9. exits nonzero if any entry cannot be parsed ✓ (S15)\n10. can be wired into pre-commit hook without much effort ✓ (S16)\n11. older entries for previous schema automatically migrated ✓ (S17)\n12. entries referencing retired regions dropped and logged as warnings ✓ (S18–S19)\n\nHmm — S17: \"The system automatically migrated older entries written for the previous schema.\" Wait — is \"migrated\" used transitively correct? \"The system automatically migrated older entries\" — yes, the system performed migration on the entries. Original: \"Older entries ... were automatically migrated\" — passive; agent implied (the daemon/system). Fine.\n\nOne concern: \"written\" in S17 — \"entries written for the previous schema\" — reduced relative clause, active-ish, not banned (no was/were/been before it). ✓\n\nNow check rule 2 across entire text: search for \"was\", \"were\", \"been\". My text: no \"was\", no \"were\", no \"been\". Let me scan: upgraded, Before, lived, merged, reads, caches, stays, restarts, receives, used, advised, should, run, slip, falls, measured, cost, prints, exits, cannot, parse, wire, migrated, dropped, logged. No was/were/been. ✓\n\nAlso double-check hyphenated tokens: \"--dry-run\" exact ✓. \"pre-commit\" ✓ (not protected but fine).\n\nAlso, is there any word ending in \"ed\" right after \"been/was/were\"? None since none present. ✓\n\nAlso check: \"advised\" — preceded by \"The team\" — fine.\n\nPotential edge: the checker regex might be case-insensitive and match \"Was\"/\"Were\". I have none.\n\nAnother possible naive-split concern: \"v4.12.0\" splits: fragment 1 = \"The team upgraded the fleet daemon to v4\" (8 tokens) ✓; fragment \"12\" (1) ✓; fragment \"0 last quarter.\" (3) ✓.\n\n\"/etc/fleetd/routes.toml\" splits: \"Staff merged those files into one file at /etc/fleetd/routes\" (9) ✓; \"toml.\" (1) ✓.\n\n\"$0.35\": \"It cost roughly $0\" (4) ✓; \"35 per thousand requests in extra upstream charges.\" (7) ✓.\n\nAll good. Also make sure no sentence fragment exceeds 20 even with odd splits. Everything well under.\n\nOne more consideration — the instruction says \"Write plain prose only: no headings, no bullet lists.\" My output is a plain paragraph. Good.\n\nShould it be one paragraph? Yes, plain prose. I'll write it as a single paragraph.\n\nAlso \"It then caches the routing table in memory.\" — original: \"the routing table ... is read once at startup and then cached in memory\". ✓\n\nAlso fact: file \"is read once at startup\" — I said \"The process reads that file once at startup.\" ✓\n\nAlso \"until the process is restarted or a reload signal is received from the supervisor\" — mine: \"until the process restarts or receives a reload signal from the supervisor.\" ✓\n\nNow write rewritten.txt. Then verify with a quick script maybe. Let me write the file and run a verification script to double-check the rules (sentence word counts and passive regex). That's prudent.\n\nActually the task says a script checks them — I can write my own quick check to validate. Let me do it with python3 or node. I'll use a simple regex:\n\npassive = r\"\\b(was|were|been)\\b(\\s+\\w+ly)?\\s+\\w+ed\\b\"\n\nAnd sentence split: split on [.!?] and count whitespace tokens per fragment. Check ≤20 for both real sentences and naive fragments (naive fragments are what a strict checker might use). Since real-sentence split vs naive split — the safest is to check both.\n\nLet me finalize the text:\n\n\"The team upgraded the fleet daemon to v4.12.0 last quarter. Before that, the routing table lived in several scattered files. Staff merged those files into one file at /etc/fleetd/routes.toml. The process reads that file once at startup. It then caches the routing table in memory. The cache stays valid until the process restarts or receives a reload signal from the supervisor. Operators used to edit the old files directly. The team advised these operators to validate each change first. They should run the reload command with the --dry-run flag. A malformed entry can slip past a direct reload. Then every worker in the pool falls back to the default route. The incident review measured that fallback. It cost roughly $0.35 per thousand requests in extra upstream charges. The reload command prints a diff of the routes that would change. It exits with a nonzero status when it cannot parse an entry. You can wire it into a pre-commit hook without much effort. The system automatically migrated older entries written for the previous schema. The system dropped entries that referenced retired regions. It logged each drop as a warning.\"\n\nHmm, one thing: \"Operators used to edit the old files directly.\" — \"used to\" is fine.\n\nAlso, minor style: two sentences starting \"The system\" consecutively — fine, plain prose.\n\nAlso should I merge S18 and S19? \"The system dropped entries that referenced retired regions and logged warnings.\" — meaning: dropped the entries, and logged (the drops) as warnings. Original \"dropped and logged as warnings\" — ambiguous what was logged. My two sentences say: dropped entries; logged each drop as a warning. That preserves the meaning well.\n\nWord check S15: \"It exits with a nonzero status when it cannot parse an entry.\" — It(1) exits(2) with(3) a(4) nonzero(5) status(6) when(7) it(8) cannot(9) parse(10) an(11) entry.(12) = 12 ✓\n\nS16: You(1) can(2) wire(3) it(4) into(5) a(6) pre-commit(7) hook(8) without(9) much(10) effort.(11) = 11 ✓\n\nS6: 16 words ✓ (under 20).\n\nOK. Now, also double-check the phrase \"can slip past a direct reload\" — original: \"a malformed entry that was not caught before the reload\". \"slip past a direct reload\" captures \"not caught before the reload\". Good enough? Maybe better: \"A malformed entry can evade checks before a reload.\" Hmm, \"direct reload\" — the original means: if you reload without dry-run validation, the malformed entry goes through. Mine says \"can slip past a direct reload\" — meaning a reload done directly (without validation). Acceptable. Alternative: \"A malformed entry can survive a reload.\" I'll keep \"slip past a direct reload\" — hmm, \"direct reload\" might be confusing. Original: \"any change should first be validated ... because a malformed entry that was not caught before the reload would cause...\". So the reload happens; if the entry is malformed and not caught beforehand, workers fall back. My \"A malformed entry can slip past a direct reload\" implies the reload happens and the entry slips through. Then \"Then every worker in the pool falls back to the default route.\" ✓ causal link preserved.\n\nMaybe clearer: \"A malformed entry can pass a reload without detection.\" Hmm. Or \"A malformed entry can reach the reload without detection.\" I'll keep \"slip past a direct reload\" — it's plain and clear.\n\nHmm, actually let me reconsider: \"direct reload\" vs \"reload without a check\". \"A malformed entry can slip past an unchecked reload.\" — clearer. 9 words: A(1) malformed(2) entry(3) can(4) slip(5) past(6) an(7) unchecked(8) reload.(9) ✓. \"unchecked reload\" = reload without dry-run. Good.\n\nThen S11: \"Then every worker in the pool falls back to the default route.\" ✓\n\nAlso, one more style check for ASD-STE100-ish (the global instruction): active voice, ≤20 words, one idea per sentence, simple tenses. My draft complies mostly. \"Then\" starting a sentence is okay. \"roughly\" — fine.\n\nWait — global AGENTS.md says writing style rules apply to user-facing text. The file content here is the deliverable the user asked to produce with specific rules; the task rules (≤20 words per sentence, etc.) govern. My reply must be \"DONE\" — and any other text I add should follow STE style and be minimal. The task says reply DONE.\n\nNow write the file and run a verification script.\n\nVerification script (python3):\n\n```python\nimport re\ntext = open('rewritten.txt').read()\n# naive sentence split\nfrags = re.split(r'[.!?]', text)\nfor f in frags:\n    toks = f.split()\n    if len(toks) > 20: print(\"LONG\", len(toks), f)\n# passive\nm = re.findall(r'\\b(?:was|were|been)\\b(?:\\s+\\w+ly)?\\s+\\w+ed\\b', text, re.I)\nprint(\"passive:\", m)\n# tokens present\nfor t in ['/etc/fleetd/routes.toml', '--dry-run', 'v4.12.0', '$0.35']:\n    print(t, t in text)\n```\n\nAlso verify real-sentence split with whitespace token counting — same as naive. And check \"was|were|been\" absence separately:\n\nre.findall(r'\\b(was|were|been)\\b', text) → should be empty.\n\nAlso check source.txt unchanged (I won't touch it).\n\nLet me proceed: write rewritten.txt, then run checks.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_111a45927001gAVuTMLYe9dnVW","sessionID":"ses_eee5d3465ffeQIkXyaRSImUGGH","messageID":"msg_111